# TODO — game OS, milestones M0..M6
# "Every line known. Minimum CPU. Minimum RAM. Zero GPU driver."

## NORTH STAR

An OS that boots straight into a shell, lets me write a program in my own
assembly language inside the OS, and runs it as a game at 60 fps.
Installs on real hardware later. I can read and explain every line.

M6 = "a playable game written in my own asm, on my own OS, at 60 fps".
Nothing after M6 is planned here on purpose.

---

## RESOURCE BUDGET (hard limits, measure every milestone)

| Target                  | Limit            | Why it is achievable                            |
|-------------------------|------------------|-------------------------------------------------|
| Kernel binary           | <= 32 KiB        | no libc, no C++, no tables you don't use        |
| Whole OS image          | <= 96 KiB        | boot + stage2 + kernel, no assets baked in      |
| RAM used at idle        | <= 256 KiB       | static arenas, no per-frame allocation          |
| Boot to shell           | <= 300 ms (QEMU) | no ACPI, no probing, no init ordering           |
| CPU at idle             | ~0 %             | HLT in the idle loop, timer off when unused     |
| CPU while game runs     | <= 60 % of 1 core| fixed-point math, dirty rects, no overdraw      |
| Frame time @1024x768x32 | <= 16.6 ms       | measured per stage, not guessed                 |
| GPU usage               | 0 driver         | one mode set at boot, then MMIO stores only     |
| Heap allocations/frame  | 0                | all buffers preallocated at startup             |

Rules that deliver the budget:
- Compile: `-Os -ffreestanding -fno-stack-protector -fno-pic -fno-pie
  -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -mno-red-zone -mno-sse -mno-mmx
  -mgeneral-regs-only` and link `-nostdlib -static`.
- No floating point. Fixed-point (16.16) for everything: no FPU state in
  context switches, no soft-float blobs, deterministic.
- No malloc in the kernel hot paths. Static arenas + a bump allocator;
  free lists only where really needed.
- Idle = `hlt`. Input = interrupts. Never poll a device in a loop with a spin.
- Draw only what changed: dirty rectangles / dirty rows, never full redraw.
- Prefer hardware help when it is free: VGA text mode costs 0 CPU and 0 RAM.
- Measure with `rdtsc` and a `perf` shell command. If it is not measured it
  does not count as optimisation.

---

## FROZEN CONTRACTS (do not change without writing an ADR in docs/)

1. Syscall ABI       — one entry, numbered calls, versioned      (M2)
2. Executable format — header + sections + entry point           (M3)
3. Pak format        — magic, version, entry count, offsets      (M3)
4. Mini-FS layout    — superblock, inode table, data blocks      (M3)
5. Bytecode ISA      — this is "my assembly", fixed opcodes      (M5)
6. Driver contract   — probe/init/irq/read/write, no globals     (later)

---

## M0 — DISPLAY  [DONE]
Goal: prove the boot chain works.
Built: stage1 (512 B) -> int 0x13 -> stage2 -> VGA text via 0xB8000 + cursor.
Acceptance: green text on screen, 55 AA signature, image builds via make.

## M1 — CPU CONTROL
Goal: own the CPU. No BIOS after this point.
Build:
  - protected mode switch in stage2 (cli -> A20 -> lgdt -> CR0.PE -> far jmp)
  - GDT: null, 32-bit code, 32-bit data
  - IDT: 256 entries; exceptions 0..31 handled (print name + halt)
  - remap PIC to 0x20..0x2F; mask everything except keyboard (IRQ1)
  - PIT at 100 Hz (used only for the clock; maskable later)
  - PS/2 keyboard driver: IRQ1 -> ring buffer -> key events
  - thin C kernel at 0x10000 called from asm; `puts` in C
  - idle loop is `hlt`
Acceptance:
  - typing echoes on screen via IRQ, not polling
  - `perf` shows idle CPU near 0 % (QEMU monitor `info cpus` steady)
  - divide-by-zero and invalid-opcode print their name and halt cleanly
Budget: kernel <= 16 KiB here.
Watch out: IDT gates need the right DPL and a 32-bit far return (`iret`),
and every ISR must `cld` + save/restore the right registers.

## M2 — MEMORY + ABIs
Goal: real memory management, and freeze the personality of the OS.
Build:
  - port to long mode (x86_64) at the START of this milestone, before paging
    exists, so paging is written once: GDT + 4-level tables + identity map
    the first 1 GiB using 2 MiB pages (2 tables, not 512)
  - static arena from the linker end symbol to a fixed ceiling
  - bitmap heap on top of the arena (4 KiB blocks), debug checks in dev builds
  - page fault handler that prints CR2 and the error bits, then halts
  - syscall ABI v1: single `int 0x80`-style gate, rax = call number,
    rdi/rsi/rdx = args. Calls: exit, write(console), read(key), tick, mmap.
  - `mem` shell-less debug print: free bytes, used bytes, largest block
Acceptance:
  - kernel runs in long mode, paging on, framebuffer/text still reachable
  - heap survives 10 000 alloc/free pairs with fragmentation reported
  - page fault on a wild pointer prints the address instead of rebooting
Budget: kernel <= 32 KiB, idle RAM <= 256 KiB.
Watch out: in long mode there is no `pushad`, `iret` is `iretq`, and GDT
entries must stay 8-byte aligned. Stack must be 16-byte aligned at calls.

## M3 — STORAGE + TERMINAL
Goal: a usable machine. Type commands, read and write files.
Build:
  - mini-FS on raw sectors (frozen layout): superblock + inode table
    + data blocks. No free-list journalling, no permissions, no directories
    deeper than one level.
  - block driver: ATA PIO (28-bit LBA), read + write, one sector at a time
  - terminal: line editor (backspace, cursor), history, command table:
      help  clear  mem  perf  ls  cat  edit  asm  run  reboot
  - text output: still VGA text mode (0 CPU, 0 RAM cost)
Acceptance:
  - create a file from the shell, reboot, `cat` it back
  - `perf` prints boot time, idle CPU, free RAM
  - every command is a table entry, not an if-chain
Budget: shell binary <= 8 KiB.
Watch out: ATA PIO needs the 400 ns delay reads between status polls; writing
without `cache flush` (0xE7) loses the file on reset.

## M4 — EDITOR
Goal: write source code on the OS itself.
Build:
  - full-screen editor on the text grid, own key handling
  - gap buffer (one allocation, O(1) insert at the cursor)
  - horizontal scroll, status line (file, line, column, dirty flag)
  - commands: save, quit, goto line, save-as
  - redraw only changed rows (dirty row bitmap)
Acceptance:
  - type a 200-line file with no visible lag (keystroke -> screen <= 1 frame)
  - only dirty rows are rewritten (verify with a counters build)
  - open, edit, save, reopen: content identical
Budget: editor <= 12 KiB RAM for a 200-line file.
Watch out: cursor arithmetic off-by-one at the end of a line, and tabs. Pick
tab = 4 spaces and never look back.

## M5 — ASSEMBLER + VM  (the compiler that belongs inside the OS)
Goal: write and run a program, in my own assembly, inside my own OS.
Build:
  - ISA spec in docs/ISA.md, frozen: 16 x 32-bit registers, fixed 32-bit
    instructions, memory as one flat byte array.
    Required ops: mov (reg/imm), add sub and or xor shl shr, load/store
    byte+word, jmp jz jnz jlt, call ret, push pop, mul (fixed-point),
    hlt, out (draw pixel/rect), in (key).
  - assembler: labels, comments, hex/dec literals, db, .org, immediate and
    register operands, one pass + patch table
  - VM: fetch/decode/execute loop, 1 MiB of VM memory, instruction budget
    guard so a bad program cannot hang the machine
  - `asm file.asm` -> bytecode file, `run file.bin` -> executes it
Acceptance:
  - a program that prints, loops, calls a subroutine and reads keys runs
  - Turing complete: prove it with a loop + conditional + memory (e.g. a
    tiny state machine or Collatz in asm)
  - infinite loop is caught by the budget guard, VM exits, shell returns
Budget: assembler + VM <= 16 KiB; VM interprets >= 5 M instr/s in QEMU.
Watch out: label resolution order, and signed vs unsigned in `jlt`.

## M6 — FRAMEBUFFER + ENGINE  (the gaming milestone)
Goal: the game. 60 fps, written in the asm from M5.
Build:
  - VBE mode 0x118 (1024x768x32) set once in stage2 (fallback: 800x600x32)
  - linear framebuffer pointer handed to the kernel, no GPU driver, MMIO only
  - 8x8 font blitter so the shell and editor run on the framebuffer too
  - dirty-rect compositor: back buffer + only changed tiles are blitted
  - sprite blitter with clip + palette-free direct RGB
  - engine loop: fixed 60 Hz step, input, update, render, `hlt` between frames
  - one game, written in my asm, using the ISA from M5
Acceptance:
  - game at 60 fps, frame time measured and printed by `perf`
  - CPU during gameplay <= 60 % of one core (QEMU)
  - shell and editor still work after quitting the game
  - no heap allocation after the game starts
Budget: full-screen clear <= 2 ms, sprite blit <= 1 us per 16x16 sprite.
Watch out: setting the mode after entering protected mode needs a real-mode
trampoline (BIOS INT 10h). Either set it in stage2 (preferred, simplest) or
write the trampoline as an exercise — do not call BIOS from long mode.

---

## DO NOT BUILD BEFORE M6

USB, ACPI, SMP, networking, audio, GPU drivers, a C compiler, multitasking,
processes, virtual memory isolation, filesystems on disk, UEFI, package
manager, GUI toolkit, multiple games, a debugger.
Each one is a project. Each one is parked on purpose.

---

## HABITS

- Commit after every working rung. A boot regression is silent.
- Debug with `qemu -d int,cpu_reset -D qemu.log`, not with eyes.
- `make check` after every build: size, signature, layout.
- Write the "why" in docs/ when a decision is made (ADR), especially before
  changing anything in FROZEN CONTRACTS.
- One vertical slice per milestone: demo or it did not happen.

## AFTER M6 (parked)

M7 GPT + own installer (installs on real hardware)
M8 UEFI loader (installs on any machine)
M9 JIT: bytecode -> x86_64 native
M10 audio mixer, second game, self-hosted toolchain
